    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p16-q41-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the value of the function at $1,2,\\ldots,11$, i.e. $f(1),f(2),\\ldots,f(11)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(1)=1$, $f(2)=0$, $f(3)=2$, $f(4)=0$, $f(5)=1$, $f(6)=0$, $f(7)=2$, $f(8)=1$, $f(9)=1$, $f(10)=1$, $f(11)=3$.",
          "worked": "Count the e’s in each number name:\n\n$f(1)=1$ (one), $f(2)=0$ (two), $f(3)=2$ (three), $f(4)=0$ (four), $f(5)=1$ (five), $f(6)=0$ (six), $f(7)=2$ (seven), $f(8)=1$ (eight), $f(9)=1$ (nine), $f(10)=1$ (ten), $f(11)=3$ (eleven).",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q41-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the smallest value of $a$ such that $f(a)=4$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a=17$.\n\nSeventeen contains four e’s; no smaller positive integer does.",
          "worked": "Part (a) shows that the names for $1$–$11$ contain at most three e’s. Twelve, thirteen, fourteen, fifteen and sixteen each contain two e’s. Seventeen contains four e’s, so the smallest input is $a=17$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q41-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Find a possible value of $b$ such that $f(b)=5$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For example, $b=111$.\n\n“One hundred and eleven” contains five e’s.",
          "worked": "In “one hundred and eleven”, one contributes one e, hundred contributes one and eleven contributes three.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(111)&=1+1+3\\\\f(111)&=5\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $b=111$ is a possible value.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q41-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Explain why it is hard to find the range of $f$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "There are infinitely many positive integers, with number names of unbounded length, so checking a finite list cannot establish every possible e-count.",
          "worked": "The range contains all possible e-counts. There are infinitely many positive integers, and their English names have no fixed maximum length. Checking only finitely many names cannot establish every possible output.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      }
    ]
  },
